  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 310 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: As rainfall becomes more showery in the wake of the cold \nfront, the northwesterly flow regime will support upslope rain \nand/or snow showers across the western slopes of the Adirondacks and \nGreen Mountains. Locations above 2000 feet continue to look like the \nbest spots for up to an inch of snow accumulation while a few \nflurries could be seen as low as 1500 feet. Overall impacts will be \nminimal and likely confined to grassy surfaces as the ground \ntemperatures will remain above freezing this morning. Today will be \nquite the transition to a more winter-like pattern as temperatures \nstruggle to climb into the upper 30s to mid 40s while winds remain \ngusty in the 20 to 30 mph range, especially this morning. This will \nlead to wind chills in the teens to mid 30s throughout the day with \nwind chills as low as the single digits above and below zero across \nthe high peaks this morning. If you plan to be outdoors today, \nplease dress and prepare for the weather as this dramatic cold and \nwind may be surprising to many! Tonight will be the coldest night of \nthe week as winds finally abate and skies clear with lows falling \ninto the upper teens and 20s. A rebound to more seasonal normal high \ntemperatures is expected for Sunday through Tuesday as high pressure \nbuilds overhead with highs warming into the 50s and lower 60s, \nthough nighttime lows should stay chilly in the 20s and 30s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Mid/upper lvl ridge prevails through mid week, before \nweak cold front drops south acrs our cwa on Thurs with slightly \ncooler temps. The mid/upper lvl ridge acrs the NE CONUS, should keep \nmoisture associated with coastal system well south of our cwa this \nweek, which is supported by most 00z guidance and ensemble data. The \nweak cold frnt on Thurs has very limited moisture with best dynamics \npassing well north of our cwa, so have continued a dry fcst. A brief \nsprinkle or light shower is possible over the trrn with this \nfeature, but confidence and probability is too low to mention attm. \nGiven the dry conditions, warmer temperatures and sunshine, \nsignificant drying of fine fuels, including leaf litter can be \nanticipated this upcoming week, but winds are expected to remain \nlight. High temps range from the mid 50s to mid 60s, except slightly \ncooler Thurs/Friday with lows upper 20s to lower 40s most mornings. \nNo impactful or significant weather is anticipated this upcoming \nweek or into next weekend attm.",
